<div id="mntl-sc-block-callout-body_1-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block-callout-body mntl-text-block"><ul><li>Japanese adjectives come in two main types: i-adjectives and na-adjectives.</li><li>Japanese adjectives can function like verbs, different from English adjectives.</li><li>Both i-adjectives and na-adjectives modify nouns and precede them in sentences.</li></ul></div></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_2-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> There are two distinct types of&nbsp;<a href="https://www.thoughtco.com/useful-japanese-adjectives-2027924" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="1">adjectives</a>&nbsp;in Japanese:&nbsp;i-adjectives&nbsp;and&nbsp;na-adjectives. I-adjectives all end in "~ i," though they never end in "~ ei" (e.g. "kirei" is not considered an i-adjective.)
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_3-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"><div id="mntl-block_2-0" class="comp mntl-block"><div id="ref-billboard1-sticky_1-0" class="comp ref-billboard1-sticky mntl-block"><div id="mm-ads-sc-sticky-square-ad_1-0" class="comp mm-ads-sc-sticky-square-ad mm-ads-mobSquareFlex1-sticky mm-ads-sc-sticky-square scads-to-load right-rail__item scads-ad-placed" data-height="600" style="height: auto;"><div id="mm-ads-sc-sticky-square-ad_2-0" class="comp mm-ads-sc-sticky-square-ad mm-ads-square mm-ads-gpt-adunit gpt square " data-ad-width="300" data-ad-height="250">
