</div></div></div></div></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_4-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> Japanese adjectives differ significantly from their English counterparts (and from their counterparts in other Western languages). Although Japanese adjectives have functions to modify nouns like English adjectives, they also function as verbs when used as predicates.
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_5-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_6-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> This is a concept that will take some getting used to.
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_7-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_8-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> For example, "takaiï¼ˆé«˜ã„ï¼‰" in the sentence "takai kuruma&nbsp;ï¼ˆé«˜ã„è»Šï¼‰" means, "expensive". "Takaiï¼ˆé«˜ã„ï¼‰" of "kono kuruma wa takai&nbsp;ï¼ˆã“ã®è»Šã¯é«˜ã„ï¼‰" means not just "expensive" but "is expensive".
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_9-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_10-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> When i-adjectives are used as predicates, they may be followed by "~ desuï¼ˆï½ã§ã™ï¼‰" to indicate a formal style. "Takai desu&nbsp;ï¼ˆé«˜ã„ã§ã™ï¼‰" also means, "is expensive" but it is more formal than "takai&nbsp;ï¼ˆé«˜ã„ï¼‰".
</p>
