<p id="mntl-sc-block_26-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> As mentioned above, adjectives in Japanese can function like verbs. Therefore, they conjugate just like&nbsp;<a href="https://www.thoughtco.com/how-to-conjugate-japanese-verbs-4058457" data-component="link" data-source="inlineLink" data-type="internalLink" data-ordinal="1">verbs</a>&nbsp;(but probably much more simply). This concept can be confusing for first-time students of the Japanese language.&nbsp;
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_27-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_28-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> <strong>Informal</strong>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_29-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_30-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> Present Negative:&nbsp;Replace the final&nbsp;<strong>~ i</strong>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<strong>~ ku nai</strong>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_31-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_32-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> Past:&nbsp;Replace the final&nbsp;<strong>~ i</strong>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<strong>~ katta</strong>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_33-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_34-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> Past Negative: Replace the final&nbsp;<strong>~ i</strong>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<strong>~ ku nakatta</strong>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_35-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_36-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> <strong>Formal</strong>
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_37-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_38-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> Add&nbsp;<strong>~desu</strong>&nbsp;to all of the informal forms.
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_39-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
<p id="mntl-sc-block_40-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> There is also a variation in the formal negative forms.<br>* Negative: Replace&nbsp;<strong>~i</strong>&nbsp;with&nbsp;<strong>~ku arimasen</strong><br>* Past Negative: Add&nbsp;<strong>~ deshita</strong>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<strong>~ku arimasen&nbsp;</strong><br>These negative forms are considered slightly more polite than others.
</p>
<div id="mntl-sc-block_41-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-adslot mntl-block"></div>
