<p id="mntl-sc-block_51-0" class="comp mntl-sc-block mntl-sc-block-html"> These are&nbsp;called na-adjectives because "~ na" marks this group of adjectives when directly modifying nouns (e.g. yuumeina gaka). Unlike i-adjectives, na-adjectives cannot be used as predicates themselves. When a na-adjective is used as a predicate, the final "na" is deleted and followed by either "~ da" or "~ desu (in formal speech)". As with nouns, "~ da" or "~ desu" changes the word's form to express the past tense, the negative&nbsp;and the affirmative.
</p>
